        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "d564d29d-37fc-5c3d-be3a-82c78673a39a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Gustave-Hippolyte Roger, Hector Berlioz, Giacomo Meyerbeer, Daniel Auber, Frederick Ashton, Constant Lambert, William Chappell\n- Works: La damnation de Faust, Le prophète, L'enfant prodigue, Les Rendezvous\n- Places: Paris, Opéra-Comique\n\nFacts:\n- In 1846, La damnation de Faust was first performed at the Opéra-Comique in Paris on 6 December 1846.\n- In 1846, Gustave-Hippolyte Roger created the leading tenor role in La damnation de Faust by Hector Berlioz.\n- Gustave-Hippolyte Roger sang the role of Azaël in the premiere of Daniel Auber's L'enfant prodigue.\n- In 1849, Gustave-Hippolyte Roger created the leading tenor role in Giacomo Meyerbeer's Le prophète.\n- In 1933, Les Rendezvous was created as a plotless one-act ballet with choreography by Frederick Ashton to music by Daniel Auber from L'enfant prodigue arranged by Constant Lambert, and with designs by William Chappell.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "19324b9f-4cfb-5c90-8456-97b67a35989a",
        "text": "Page: Gustave-Hippolyte Roger. Sentence: He is best known for creating the leading tenor roles in \"La damnation de Faust\" by Berlioz in 1846 and Meyerbeer's \"Le prophète\" in 1849.\nPage: La damnation de Faust. Sentence: It was first performed at the Opéra-Comique in Paris on 6 December 1846.\nPage: L'enfant prodigue (Auber). Sentence: The role of Azaël (the prodigal son of the title) was sung in the premiere by the celebrated French tenor, Gustave-Hippolyte Roger, (1815-1879).\nPage: Les Rendezvous. Sentence: Les Rendezvous is a plotless one-act ballet created in 1933, with choreography by Frederick Ashton to the music of Daniel Auber (the ballet music from his opera \"L'enfant prodigue\") arranged by Constant Lambert and with designs by William Chappell.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
